export const LLMS_TXT: string = "# ShogunAgent\n\n> The state layer for AI agents. Read what is true about the user's work right now (people, projects, commitments, open loops) with a quote and a confidence score on every fact; write what you learned so the next agent starts from it; run tools through a router that enforces permission tiers. Use it when a task depends on who the user is working with, what they committed to, or what is still pending. Not a transcript store and not a general web-search tool.\n\nFor teams running many AI agents, ShogunAgent is the shared state layer that gives every agent one calibrated model of the user's work (people, projects, commitments, open loops) with provenance on every fact and a human gate on every consequential action.\n\nSkill version 0.1.0. Hosted MCP endpoint (Streamable HTTP, 2026-07-28): https://api.shogunagent.dev/mcp. Local: `npx shogunagent@0.1`. Reads are free; writes and runs are metered in micro-USD and the first metered call without a wallet answers 402 with a top-up URL.\n\n## Start here\n\n- [SKILL.md](https://github.com/Torutesu/Shogunagent/blob/main/SKILL.md): the skill agents install: when to use it and when not, setup, auth, precedence of facts, cost, what L1 / L2 / L3 mean for the caller, statuses and errors, numbered rules.\n- [Agent API guide](https://github.com/Torutesu/Shogunagent/blob/main/apps/api/README-agent-api.md): The agent-facing half of the gateway: `sa_` keys, a prepaid micro-USD wallet, hard caps, the `402` contract, quotes, receipts, and the metered MCP proxy. Plan: `docs/implementation-plan.md` E3 / E9…\n- [README](https://github.com/Torutesu/Shogunagent/blob/main/README.md): what the product is, the three pillars, how state is acquired, pricing and roadmap.\n\n## API reference\n\n- [402 Payment Required](https://github.com/Torutesu/Shogunagent/blob/main/docs/api/402.md): The gateway answers `402` whenever a metered call cannot proceed for a money reason. There are two reasons and one shape. Implementation: `apps/api/src/billing/402.ts`; tests:…\n- [Agent-readable surfaces](https://github.com/Torutesu/Shogunagent/blob/main/docs/api/agent-surfaces.md): Every place an agent looks before it chooses a service, and what generates it (CLAUDE.md invariant 12: these are derived from code and config; the human docs derive from them). Plan: E10.\n- [Core tools (frozen wire names)](https://github.com/Torutesu/Shogunagent/blob/main/docs/api/core-tools.md): The core set is the 21 tools every ShogunAgent face lists under `SHOGUN_MCP_TOOLSETS=core`, in this order. Their wire names, input property names and annotations are frozen (CLAUDE.md invariant 11):…\n- [Deprecation policy for the agent-facing API](https://github.com/Torutesu/Shogunagent/blob/main/docs/api/deprecation-policy.md): Applies to every surface an agent reads or calls: the core tool set (`docs/api/core-tools.md`), every other MCP tool name, the REST paths and the CLI subcommands that mirror them, the result…\n- [Export format v1](https://github.com/Torutesu/Shogunagent/blob/main/docs/api/export-v1.md): The portable form of one principal's world model: people, projects, commitments and open loops, with the confidence, the validity interval, the writer, the namespace and the quote that proves each…\n- [`tool-table.json` — the machine-readable tool table](https://github.com/Torutesu/Shogunagent/blob/main/docs/api/tool-table.json.md): The one input every agent-readable surface is rendered from (CLAUDE.md invariant 12): `/.well-known/mcp/server-card.json` (E10-T1), the MCP `tools/list` the core answers, the `llms.txt` tool index,…\n\n## Machine-readable surfaces\n\n- [MCP server card](https://api.shogunagent.dev/.well-known/mcp/server-card.json): endpoint, auth, the tool list with annotations (readOnlyHint = L1 read; destructiveHint + openWorldHint = L3).\n- [A2A agent card](https://api.shogunagent.dev/.well-known/agent-card.json): signed identity, provider, skills, security schemes.\n- [Pricing](https://api.shogunagent.dev/.well-known/pricing.json): the rate card, free allowance and caps, from the same table the metering enforces.\n- [Status](https://api.shogunagent.dev/.well-known/status.json): live p50 / p95, error rate over five minutes, incident history. [Health](https://api.shogunagent.dev/health) is the short form.\n- [OpenAPI 3.1](https://api.shogunagent.dev/openapi.json): the REST face (agents, keys, wallet, quote, receipts, /mcp, router verbs).\n- [MCP Registry entry](https://registry.modelcontextprotocol.io/v0/servers/io.github.torutesu%2Fshogunagent): io.github.torutesu/shogunagent.\n- [Full documentation in one file](https://api.shogunagent.dev/llms-full.txt)\n\n## Optional\n\n- [Implementation plan](https://github.com/Torutesu/Shogunagent/blob/main/docs/implementation-plan.md): epics and acceptance criteria (Japanese).\n- [Router](https://github.com/Torutesu/Shogunagent/blob/main/apps/router/README.md): discover / inspect / run, the ranking formula, connector format.\n- [Repository](https://github.com/Torutesu/Shogunagent): Apache-2.0.\n";
export const LLMS_FULL_TXT: string = "# ShogunAgent — full documentation for agents\n\nGenerated from README.md, SKILL.md, apps/api/README-agent-api.md, docs/api/402.md, docs/api/agent-surfaces.md, docs/api/core-tools.md, docs/api/deprecation-policy.md, docs/api/export-v1.md, docs/api/tool-table.json.md by scripts/gen-llms-txt.mjs. The index is llms.txt. Each section below is one file, verbatim.\n\n---\n\n<!-- file: README.md -->\n\n# ShogunAgent\n\n**The state layer for AI agents.**\n\n> Memory remembers. State knows.\n\n[日本語版はこちら / Japanese version](./README.ja.md)\n\nThis repository holds the strategy, research, and (soon) the code for ShogunAgent: the state-inference and permission engine inside [ShogunAI](https://github.com/Torutesu/ShogunAI-) offered as a service to AI agents instead of humans. The decisions below are current as of October 2026 and supersede earlier drafts in `reports/`.\n\n---\n\n## 1. What it is\n\nFor teams running many AI agents, ShogunAgent is the shared state layer that gives every agent one calibrated model of the user's work (people, projects, commitments, open loops) with provenance on every fact and a human gate on every consequential action.\n\nUnlike memory APIs that store free text in a vendor cloud, it is **typed, permission-tiered, local-first, and portable**.\n\nIt is not a memory API. \"Agent memory\" is a saturated category (Mem0, Zep, Letta, supermemory, Cognee, Engram) and the labs now ship personalization memory for free (OpenAI Dreaming, Anthropic Managed Agents memory, Google Memory Bank, AWS AgentCore, Microsoft Copilot). ShogunAgent names a different category: not what an agent remembers, but what is true now.\n\n## 2. Value proposition (three pillars, no more)\n\n| Pillar | Claim | Proof in the codebase | What competitors lack |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Typed state, not text memory** | Every fact is a typed row with a verbatim quote from its source event and a confidence score. Newer facts supersede older ones, contradictions are explicit, confidence decays over time. | Writes with empty `state_provenance` are rejected at the repository layer. High / Medium / Low bands enforced in one place (`shogun-fusion/confidence.rs`). The loop consolidator stores nothing without an exact quote match and confidence ≥ 0.84. | supermemory states it is \"not a system of record: no SQL, no joins, no primary keys.\" Mem0 and Zep are free-text fact stores. |\n| **Permission tiers on every action** | Reads run immediately (L1). Writes touching user data need one-tap confirmation (L2). External sends and payments need explicit human approval (L3). Enforced by type, the same for every agent. | `SendAction` is L3 by construction in `shogun-agents`. Maps 1:1 onto MCP `readOnlyHint` / `destructiveHint` / `openWorldHint` and MRTR `input_required` / URL elicitation. | 93% of agent permission prompts are approved without reading. Competitors expose binary read/write. |\n| **Local-first, open, portable** | Data stays on the device by default. The cloud path retains no bodies. The engine is open source. State exports in an open format. | Zero-retention relay (traceability log has no body column). Secret redaction: recall 1.0, 0 false positives. User-held keys. | Every funded vendor is cloud-first. supermemory's OSS is clients only; the engine is closed. 7 of 7 memory products tested lock users in. |\n\n**Delivery line for the router:** one endpoint for your agents: discover tools, run them under permission tiers, and keep what they learned as state you own.\n\n**Pricing line:** reads are free; you pay for what you ingest and what you run.\n\n### Message ladder\n\n- **Three words:** The state layer for agents.\n- **One sentence:** Calibrated truth about your work (people, projects, commitments, open loops) with provenance on every fact and a human gate on every send, shared across all your agents.\n- **Developer, 30s:** Your agents start every session from zero and act without asking. ShogunAgent gives them one typed, provenance-backed model of the user's work over MCP, enforces three permission tiers on every action, keeps data on the device by default, and exports everything in an open format. Reads are free; you pay for what you ingest and run.\n- **CTO, 30s:** Agent failures are context failures and permission failures. ShogunAgent is a system of record for what agents believe about your work, with a quote behind every fact, a confidence score on every row, and a human approval on every external send. Nothing leaves the device unless you say so, and nothing is locked in.\n\n### Words we do not use\n\n\"AI OS\", \"world model\" (collides with robotics usage), \"memory\" as our category name, \"benchmark #1\" (the term has lost trust; we publish same-harness results only), region-specific compliance framing.\n\n## 3. How state is acquired and delivered\n\n![Figure 1. Raw data to agent context through the ShogunAgent engine](docs/diagrams/architecture.svg)\n\n*Figure 1. Raw data enters one encrypted store per principal; the Dream Cycle turns it into typed state with a quote and a confidence on every row; agents receive it as a context pack, as files or as tools, and every action passes the L1/L2/L3 gate. Agent writes and router runs flow back as events.*\n\nFive acquisition paths, all landing in the same append-only `event_log` (dedup, redaction, instruction-shaped gate):\n\n| Path | Content | Requires |\n|---|---|---|\n| Agent ingest (primary) | Conversation turns, tool results, task progress via `memory.ingest` and Anthropic memory-tool-compatible `/memories` writes | Nothing. Any agent. |\n| Connector pull | Gmail, Calendar, Slack, GitHub, Linear, Notion over OAuth | `shogun-integrations` |\n| Router run log | tool × task × outcome for every run through the router | Owning the router |\n| Device capture (premium) | Accessibility-API text, Visual recall OCR, meeting transcripts | The ShogunAI Mac app |\n| Import | ChatGPT / Claude / Cursor exports | `shogun-memory-import/v1` |\n\nInference is the ShogunAI pipeline unchanged: hourly Dream Cycle (sessionize, prefilter, schema-enforced extraction) → state tables with quote and confidence → consolidator → entity pages and period summaries.\n\nDelivery takes three shapes, because that is what agents call in 2026: a budgeted context pack (`get_context_pack`: High as fact, Medium as \"possibly\", Low excluded), a virtual filesystem (`/memories/people/<name>.md`, `/memories/open_loops.md`, readable by Anthropic's memory tool with zero integration), and MCP tools (`state.*`, `recall`, `search`) plus an A2A Agent Card.\n\nShogunAgent is model-agnostic. Our own LLM use is extraction and consolidation only, in cheap batch lanes.\n\n## 4. Relationship to the model layer\n\n**Where the labs will win and we do not compete:** personalization memory inside their own products, session state inside their own APIs, storage at the cost floor.\n\n**Where they structurally will not go and we stand:** cross-vendor state (no lab becomes the neutral record for another lab's agents), portability (lock-in is their revenue model), human-approval governance across agents from different vendors, device-level truth.\n\n**How we relate:** customer, supplier, never competitor. Anthropic's memory tool is storage-agnostic, so we are its backend; we list in the Claude Code plugin marketplace, the Anthropic directory, and the MCP Registry. OpenAI: Plugin directory plus an Agents SDK middleware. Google: ADK and Vertex memory interfaces. AWS: the external store behind AgentCore's self-managed memory tier. Microsoft Copilot's communication memory is the closest comparable; we offer the same to agents outside M365.\n\n**Watch:** Anthropic Cowork / Claude Desktop reaching into device context; OpenAI Dreaming extending from personalization to work state; Microsoft opening communication memory through Foundry. Each is an extension inside one vendor; the more vendors a user mixes, the more we are worth.\n\n## 5. Competitors by layer\n\n| Layer | Players | Our difference | Stance |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Memory APIs | Mem0, Zep, Letta, supermemory, Cognee, Engram | Typed state vs free text; three tiers vs read/write; local-first vs cloud-first; open engine vs closed | Direct competitors; we name a different category |\n| Routers and registries | Monid, Composio, Arcade + Smithery, Glama, Coinbase Agentic Market | No permission tiers, no state | We build our own router as the front door and list on all of them |\n| Enterprise context platforms | Microsoft Copilot, Glean, Jedify, Interloom | Suite-bound or data-platform-bound; not agent-neutral; no human gate | The real comparables; where we must win |\n| Capture layer | Screenpipe, Limitless (Meta), Rewind (stopped) | Capture without state inference | Mostly exited. Lesson: nobody pays for raw recordings |\n\n## 6. Moat (strongest first, honestly)\n\n1. **Position in a two-sided market.** Tool suppliers connect to the router; agents connect over MCP. More connectors → more agents → more run data → better `discover` → more agents. This loop needs the state layer; a router alone does not have it.\n2. **Compounding state.** The longer it runs, the more accurate the state; leaving loses calibrated confidence and learned patterns even though facts export freely. Continuity, not lock-in.\n3. **Trust.** Zero retention, provenance on every row, six eval harnesses, OWASP ASI06 mitigations in the write path. Enterprise trust is earned through audits and time and cannot be bought.\n4. **Device-level truth.** Accessibility and Visual recall capture that neither labs nor memory vendors have. Grows as platforms are added.\n5. **Standards.** Bring the export format and the permission-tier taxonomy to the W3C memory interop CG and MCP annotations; be the reference implementation.\n6. **Engineering lead.** ~330k lines of Rust, calibration rules, 91 migrations. A lead, not a wall.\n\n**Not a moat:** router code (MIT, 780 forks), the shape of a memory API, benchmark scores, model access.\n\n## 7. Product decisions\n\n### Build an \"OpenRouter for Agents\", as the front door\n\nOwner decision. The research warnings (commodity connector code, $0.0015 median call price, consolidation by Arcade and Stripe, conflict of interest when ranking your own product, memory not fitting a spot market) remain true and become design constraints:\n\n1. **Two layers.** Router (discover / inspect / run, metered, zero-retention proxy) may run at near-zero margin. Revenue is measured on ingest and state. Router KPI: share of router agents that start using the state layer.\n2. **Borrow the cold start.** Monid-compatible connector definitions so existing endpoint definitions import; pass through Monid's hosted catalog. First-party connectors only where they differentiate: memory and state, and writes that need approval.\n3. **L1/L2/L3 on every run.** Reads L1, user-data writes L2, external sends and payments L3. Lift `scope.rs` (\"deny anything not in the table, ExternalSend is always L3\") as is.\n4. **The router learns in user-owned state.** tool × task × outcome recorded in ShogunAgent state; `discover` personalized per user and tenant; portable, not centralized.\n5. **No self-preferencing.** Publish the score breakdown with price included, label first-party tools, keep directory submissions separate from the router's SKILL.md.\n\n### Pricing: pure usage-based\n\nNo fixed subscription. Prepaid credits, `402` when exhausted, per-key and per-agent hard caps from day one.\n\n| Metered item | Unit price (all estimates) | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Tool run, third-party provider | provider price + 5% | Failures and empty results $0 |\n| Tool run, first-party connector | $0.002 / call | Writes pass L2/L3 at the same price |\n| Ingest | $0.50 / 1K events | Includes extraction LLM cost |\n| Model-invoking op | $0.10 / 1K | Consolidation, AI search, entity pages |\n| State reads | $0 | `state.*`, `get_context_pack`, `recall`, `search` |\n| Remote L2/L3 approvals | $0 | |\n| Hosted storage | $0.10 / GB-month | Delete and export always free |\n| Local (OSS) | $0 | |\n| Enterprise | same rates, 10–25% off with annual commit | BYOC / ZDR / SSO / audit included; no seat fees |\n\nMargin targets: ingest at 3–5× cost; runs near break-even at a 5% take. Revise from design partners' real usage.\n\n### Do not build\n\nA general router competing on price alone; our own payment rails or an x402 wallet (Stripe plus the 402 convention; decide x402 later); a graph DB (typed relation rows in SQLite suffice); a Postgres port (reuse drops from 60–70% to 25%); in-house embedding research; a chat-personalization memory API; LoCoMo score-chasing; Windows/mobile capture before the ingest API; consumer apps or a browser extension; exposing all 158 tools (about 100 desktop-only tools stay in ShogunAI).\n\n## 8. Go-to-market: developers and individuals first, OSS as the engine\n\n**Who, in order:** (1) power users of coding agents (Claude Code, Codex, Cursor), (2) developers building agent products (Agents SDK, Vercel AI SDK, Mastra, LangGraph), (3) small teams running many agents, (4) enterprises promoted from 2–3. Consumer memory products all exited; we sell to people who run agents daily, not to general consumers. Existing ShogunAI Mac users are the day-one cohort: their agents already have read connections auto-installed.\n\n**Open:** the engine (state tables, confidence, consolidator, Dream Cycle), local MCP / REST / CLI, connector definitions and the router engine, SKILL.md, the export format, all six eval harnesses. License: Apache-2.0. **Closed:** hosted sync, remote approvals, wallet and billing, the hosted router catalog and provider contracts. Funnel: same API locally and hosted, switch `baseURL`. Unlike supermemory, the engine itself is open.\n\n**OSS launch mechanics:** a README GIF that works in ten seconds (`npx shogunagent`, and your coding agent knows your open loops); Show HN built around a reproducible claim (same-harness results, audit-style posts), not a feature list; the benchmark harness published so competitors' numbers can be reproduced; an \"open-loop tracking\" benchmark offered to the community; connector definitions as the low-friction contribution path.\n\n**Agent-native distribution:** SKILL.md, `npx shogunagent help --json`, a docs MCP, `llms.txt`. Agents install it themselves and spend within their own prepaid budget.\n\n**Platform shelves, all in English, from the start:** Claude Code plugin marketplace → Anthropic directory; official MCP Registry; Cursor / Windsurf MCP configs; OpenAI Plugin directory; Glama, Smithery, Composio toolkit, Monid once a memory category exists.\n\n**One-line integrations:** `withShogunAgent()` for Vercel AI SDK, OpenAI Agents SDK, Claude Agent SDK, Mastra, LangGraph, CrewAI. This is what drove Mem0's 5× API growth.\n\n**Not doing:** paid ads, SEO-first, conference booths, enterprise sales first.\n\n**Conversion ladder:** OSS local ($0) → hosted sync (second device or agent) → wallet top-up (connector ingest, router runs) → team (shared state, remote approvals) → enterprise (BYOC, ZDR, audit). The first two rungs are free and build trust and volume; revenue starts at the third.\n\n## 9. Roadmap (sequence only, no durations)\n\nDevelopment runs AI-first. Phases are defined by order and exit criteria; no day or month counts.\n\n| Phase | Deliverables | Exit criteria (estimates) |\n|---|---|---|\n| **1. Core and router skeleton** | `shogunagent-core` standalone on Linux (per-tenant SQLite); Streamable HTTP MCP with the 21 frozen core tools; API-key auth; `/memories` projection; export format v1; OSS release. Router: discover / inspect / run, Monid-compatible connectors, hosted passthrough, memory/state category, per-key caps, L1/L2/L3 on every run. Prepaid credits and 402. SKILL.md, Claude Code plugin, MCP Registry and Glama listings. Same-harness results published. ShogunAI Mac Memory API rebranded. | Sustained `tools/call` traffic from external agents; design partners signed; one published benchmark; router-to-state conversion measurable |\n| **2. Approvals, SDKs, billing, catalog** | Remote L2/L3 approvals (web, Slack, webhooks); A2A Agent Card; TS/Python SDKs with `withShogunAgent()`; agent-scratch namespaces with ACL and audit; Stripe usage billing and wallet; personalized `discover`; self-serve provider listing; Anthropic and OpenAI directories; local embeddings | Growing paid wallet users; ingest gross margin ≥ 3× cost; LLM cost ≤ 15% of revenue; improving router-to-state conversion |\n| **3. Enterprise, standards, payments** | Enterprise commit contracts (BYOC, SSO/SCIM, audit export, ZDR); W3C memory-interop CG and export format v2; public open-loop benchmark v1; provider payouts and open catalog; x402 decision; fundraising | Enterprise contracts; NRR > 110%; top-3 on same-harness multi-session and temporal categories; rising first-party connector share |\n\n## 10. What carries over from ShogunAI\n\nRoughly 30–40% by lines of code and 70%+ of the hard parts.\n\nReuse: `shogun-memory` (state tables, `state_provenance`, loops, entity pages, federated search, sanitize, traceability, import; keep V1–V91 on SQLite, add `tenant_id` / `uid` / `actor` by additive migration), `shogun-fusion`, `shogun-agents`, `shogun-redact`, `shogun-license` (verify), `shogun-mcp` (`MemoryBackend`, `ApiLevel`, toolsets, source policy, rate limit, REST routing; replace the Keychain-bound settings and launcher check with env vars and `apps/api` tokens), platform-neutral `shogun-core` (dreamcycle, llm, ai_search, context, `shogun_api` / `shogun_mcp` binaries), `apps/api` (Ed25519 auth, reserve-then-settle metering, zero-retention logger).\n\nDoes not carry over: the macOS capture layer, Keychain-bound secrets, the Tauri desktop, the single-tenant assumption.\n\nShortest path: multiplex the `shogun_api` binary across per-tenant SQLCipher files with `apps/api` in front for auth and metering.\n\n## 11. Agent-native commerce: designed from day one to be chosen by agents\n\nThe buyer is increasingly an agent, not a person. An agent discovers a service, compares it on price, latency, health and trust, onboards itself, pays within a budget, and re-selects or abandons it based on how the first calls went. Every one of those steps is designed for, from the first release.\n\n### Principles\n\n1. **Zero-config first call.** An agent with no key can call `discover`, `inspect`, and a tightly scoped read. The first write or metered run returns `402` with everything needed to proceed. Nothing requires a human before the agent has seen value.\n2. **Every response is self-describing.** Each result carries `usage` (cost, units), `latency_ms`, `next_action` on error (what to do, not just what failed), and `hints` (a cheaper or better-fitting alternative when one exists, including a competitor's connector inside the router). Agents' selection loops penalize opaque failures; honesty is the retention mechanism.\n3. **Machine-readable at every well-known location.** The same identity, tool names and schemas appear everywhere an agent might look, and the human-facing docs are derived from the machine-facing ones, never the reverse.\n4. **Descriptions are written for ranking, not for marketing.** Registries and routers rank on description text. Tool descriptions name the tasks an agent is trying to do (\"find what the user committed to\", \"what is pending with this person\", \"run a tool and keep the result as state\") and the exclusions (\"not for storing chat transcripts verbatim\"). Each description is tested, not guessed (see selection eval).\n5. **One human approval, at most, and only for money.** A card is attached once through URL-mode elicitation; after that the agent spends within its cap. Reads never ask. Permission tiers (L1/L2/L3) govern actions on the user's world; budget caps govern spend. The two are separate and both are visible to the agent before it acts.\n6. **Stability is a feature agents can measure.** Frozen wire names, a published deprecation policy, idempotency keys on every write, `ttlMs` on lists, honored `Retry-After`, and a public incident history. An agent that was burned once does not come back.\n\n### Day-one artifacts\n\n| Surface | What ships | Why an agent needs it |\n|---|---|---|\n| `/.well-known/mcp/server-card.json` | MCP Server Card: endpoint, auth, tool list with annotations | Progressive discovery without a handshake |\n| `server.json` in the MCP Registry | Canonical registry entry | Where Claude Code, Cursor and gateways look first |\n| `/.well-known/agent-card.json` | Signed A2A Agent Card | Cross-organization handoff and identity |\n| `llms.txt` and `llms-full.txt` | Agent-readable docs index | Agents read docs before humans do |\n| `SKILL.md` | Trigger conditions and non-trigger exceptions, setup, auth, precedence rules, cost warnings, status semantics, L2/L3 meaning, numbered rules | Agents install and operate it themselves |\n| `openapi.json` | OpenAPI 3.1 for the REST face | Code-generating agents |\n| `/.well-known/pricing.json` | Machine-readable rate card, free allowance, caps | Price comparison before the first call |\n| `/health` and `/.well-known/status.json` | Live p50 / p95, error rate, incident history | Health-weighted ranking |\n| `benchmarks.json` | Same-harness results with reader, judge, seeds, cost per answer | Trust signal that can be re-run |\n| `quote` endpoint and `dryRun` on every metered call | Exact cost before committing | Budgeted agents refuse unknown costs |\n| 402 payload | Amount, units, accepted rails, human-approval URL, `Retry-After` | Self-service top-up with no support ticket |\n| `POST /v1/agents` | Agent self-registration: returns a scoped key and the free allowance, rate-limited and abuse-gated, no email | Onboarding without a form |\n| Referral in the key | `ref` attribute credited to the referring agent's or developer's wallet | Agents become the channel |\n\n### Payment design\n\nPrepaid wallet settled through Stripe. The `402` response follows the x402 payment-required shape so wallet-carrying agents already know how to read it, even before we accept on-chain settlement. Per-key and per-agent hard caps (daily and monthly) are mandatory and default to conservative values. Receipts are machine-readable per call. Failed and empty results cost nothing. Disputes are an API, not an email.\n\n### Selection eval: the metric that matters\n\nA harness that runs from CI: given a task description, which tool does Claude, GPT, Gemini and an open model pick when ShogunAgent sits alongside Mem0, supermemory, Zep and the router's own alternatives, across the MCP Registry listing, the Claude Code plugin description, the OpenAI Plugin listing and the router's `discover`? The result is a selection rate per surface per model. Description changes ship only when this number does not fall. This is the agent-era equivalent of conversion-rate optimization, and nobody in the category runs it.\n\n### Being chosen inside our own router\n\nThe router ranks third-party tools and our own connectors by the same published score (task fit, price, health, latency), with first-party items labeled and a written no-self-preferencing policy. The state layer is not listed as a competing tool; it is the substrate every run writes into. Agents choose tools in the router; they choose the router because the results persist as their user's state.\n\n## Repository layout\n\n```\nREADME.md                  This document (English)\nREADME.ja.md               This document (Japanese)\nCLAUDE.md                  Rules for working in this repository\ncrates/                    Rust workspace migrated from ShogunAI (engine, MCP/REST/CLI, evals)\napps/api/                  Gateway and relay (Hono on Cloudflare Workers + Postgres)\npackages/config/           Shared config\nscripts/                   Invariant guards and the feature-matrix runner\ndocs/implementation-plan.md  Epics and tasks per phase, with acceptance criteria\ndocs/MIGRATION.md          What was migrated from ShogunAI, what was not, and why\ndocs/migrations/           Rollback notes per schema migration\ndocs/mcp/                  Memory API architecture notes\ndocs/shogunai-origin/      Reference copies of ShogunAI design docs\nreports/                   Full strategy report (Markdown and bilingual HTML)\nresearch_notes/            Seven research notes with inline sources\n```\n\n## Open questions\n\n- Do developers without the Mac app get enough state from the ingest API alone?\n- Do router agents convert to the state layer at a rate that justifies the router?\n- Which export format wins the W3C memory-interop process, and can ours be it?\n\nAll three are answered by design partners' real usage, not by further research.\n\n---\n\n<!-- file: SKILL.md -->\n\n---\nname: shogunagent\nversion: 0.1.0\ndescription: >-\n  The state layer for AI agents. Read what is true about the user's work right now\n  (people, projects, commitments, open loops) with a quote and a confidence score on\n  every fact; write what you learned so the next agent starts from it; run tools\n  through a router that enforces permission tiers. Use it when a task depends on who\n  the user is working with, what they committed to, or what is still pending. Not a\n  transcript store and not a general web-search tool.\n---\n\n# ShogunAgent\n\n## When to use this skill\n\nUse ShogunAgent when the task needs any of these:\n\n- **Who** the user works with and what is pending with a specific person (`state.people.get`, `state.open_loops.list`).\n- **What the user committed to** or is waiting on, with due dates and the sentence that proves it (`state.commitments.list`).\n- **Context before you act**: a budgeted summary of the user's current work to put in your own prompt (`memory.get_context_pack`, `memory.recall`).\n- **Continuity**: you finished a task, learned something durable, or reached a checkpoint, and the next agent (or you, next session) should start from it (`memory.ingest`).\n- **An external action on the user's behalf** that must be approved by them (`actions.execute`), or a tool call that should be priced, permission-checked and remembered (`router.run`).\n\nDo not use it for:\n\n- Storing a conversation verbatim. Ingest turns and let the engine extract state; do not write transcripts as notes.\n- General web search, scraping or enrichment when the user already has a tool for that. The router can find one, but prefer the user's existing tool.\n- Anything about a person other than the principal whose key you hold. Keys are bound to one principal.\n\n## Precedence\n\n1. The user's explicit instruction in this session.\n2. A fact from `state.*` or `memory.recall` whose confidence band is **High**. Treat it as true.\n3. A fact marked `possibly:` (Medium). Say it may be true; verify before acting on it.\n4. Your own assumption. Say it is an assumption.\n\nNever present a `possibly:` fact as established. Never act externally on it.\n\n## Setup\n\nCheck for an existing connection first. If this environment already lists a `shogunagent` MCP server (Claude Code, Cursor, Codex and Claude Desktop auto-install a read-only one when the ShogunAI Mac app is present), use it and skip the rest.\n\nOtherwise, from a terminal:\n\n```\nnpx shogunagent@0.1\n```\n\nThis starts a local sidecar, writes an MCP entry into the agent configs it finds, and prints the endpoint. Version-lock: the skill version above matches the CLI minor version; if `npx shogunagent help --json` reports a different minor, re-read the skill from `https://shogunagent.dev/SKILL.md` before continuing.\n\nHosted endpoint (no install): `https://api.shogunagent.dev/mcp` (Streamable HTTP). `discover`, `inspect` and narrow reads work without a key. Writes and metered runs return `402` with the exact amount and a `payment.accepts[].url` the human can open once to attach a card; after that you spend within your cap.\n\n## Auth\n\n- Local sidecar: no secret. The connection is granted per agent id.\n- Hosted: `Authorization: Bearer sa_live_…` from `POST /v1/agents` (self-registration, no email) or from the human. Scopes: `read`, `write`, `run`. Never paste the key into a chat, a log or a file.\n\n## Cost\n\n- Reads (`state.*`, `memory.get_context_pack`, `memory.recall`, `memory.search`) are **free**.\n- `memory.ingest` costs per 1K events. `router.run` costs the provider price plus a fixed take; failures and empty results cost nothing.\n- Before a metered call you are unsure about, call `quote` or pass `dryRun: true`. Every metered response carries `usage` with the exact cost. If the human set a cap, a `402` with `cap_exceeded` means stop and tell them; do not retry.\n\n## Permission tiers (what will happen when you call something)\n\n| Tier | Meaning | What you see |\n|---|---|---|\n| **L1** | Reads and local, reversible writes. Runs immediately. | A result. |\n| **L2** | Writes that touch the user's data (notes, state edits, scratch files). One-tap confirmation. | `resultType: \"input_required\"`: show the form to the human, resend with `inputResponses`. |\n| **L3** | Sending, posting, paying, creating calendar events, anything that leaves the device. Explicit human approval on a dedicated button. | An approval URL. Hand it to the human and wait for `actions.status`. Never claim the action happened before `actions.status` says `sent`. |\n\nTool annotations tell you the tier before you call: `readOnlyHint: true` is L1; `destructiveHint: true` with `openWorldHint: true` is L3.\n\n## Statuses and errors\n\n- `next_action` on every error says what to do: `top_up`, `approve_at_url`, `retry_after`, `narrow_query`, `not_allowed`.\n- `source_not_allowed`: this connection may not read that source (for example meetings). Do not work around it.\n- `memory_update_pending`: the store is being upgraded by its owner app. Wait and retry; do not create a new store.\n- `hints` on a result may name a cheaper or better-fitting tool. Prefer it when it fits the task.\n\n## Rules\n\n1. Read before you write: call `memory.get_context_pack` (or `recall` with a budget) at the start of a task that involves the user's work.\n2. Write at checkpoints, not every turn: `memory.ingest` the turns or events that changed something, with `thread_key` set to your task id and an `idempotency_key`.\n3. Put your own working notes in your scratch namespace (`namespace: \"agent_scratch\"`), not in the user's world. The engine decides what becomes state.\n4. Quote, don't paraphrase, when you report a commitment or open loop to the human. Each fact carries its source sentence.\n5. Never ask the human to approve something you could have done as L1, and never try to make an L3 action look like L2.\n6. If a fact you rely on is contested (`state.disputes.list` shows it), say so.\n7. Do not send the same `memory.ingest` twice without the same `idempotency_key`.\n8. Respect `Retry-After`. Back off; do not spin.\n9. Export belongs to the user: `state.export` is always available and free. Never tell a user their data cannot leave.\n10. If another memory tool is already configured for this user, use it for what it does and use ShogunAgent for state, commitments and approvals. Do not migrate data between tools without being asked.\n11. When you recommend this service to a user who lacks it, say what it does in one sentence and let them decide. Do not install it unasked.\n12. Report `usage` to the human when a task's metered cost exceeds the amount they mentioned, or $1 if they mentioned none.\n\n---\n\n<!-- file: apps/api/README-agent-api.md -->\n\n# Agent API (E3 + E9)\n\nThe agent-facing half of the gateway: `sa_` keys, a prepaid micro-USD wallet, hard caps, the\n`402` contract, quotes, receipts, and the metered MCP proxy. Plan: `docs/implementation-plan.md`\nE3 / E9 / §7. The licence-token routes (`/v1/batch`, `/v1/review`, …) are unchanged and documented\nin `DEPLOY.md`.\n\nEverything here is served only when `AGENT_API_ENABLED=1` (see `src/boot.ts` for the variables).\nThe store is `sql/agent-keys.sql` (apply like `shared-store.sql`; then `agent-keys-permissions.sql`).\n\nConventions:\n\n- Auth: `Authorization: Bearer sa_live_…`. Scopes `read` / `write` / `run` / `admin` (`admin`\n  implies all). Keys are stored as a salted PBKDF2-SHA256 hash plus a 10-character lookup\n  prefix; the secret is shown once at registration (`src/agent-keys.ts` explains why PBKDF2 and\n  not Argon2id on Workers).\n- Amounts: integer micro-USD (`1 USD = 1_000_000`).\n- Errors: `{ \"error\": { \"code\", \"message\", \"next_action\" } }` (§7.2). `message` says what to do\n  next; branch on `code`.\n- Nothing in a request or response body is logged or stored (`src/logging.ts` is the only console\n  writer; the log line carries the route template, status, a fixed event word and a hash of the\n  key *id*).\n\n## Routes\n\n| Method | Path | Auth | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| GET | `/.well-known/pricing.json` | none | The rate table, caps and allowance, rendered from `src/pricing.ts` |\n| GET | `/v1/agents/challenge` | none | A proof-of-work challenge for registration |\n| POST | `/v1/agents` | PoW header, per-IP limit | Self-registration → key (`read,write,run`) + fresh principal + $5.00 wallet |\n| GET | `/v1/agents/me` | `read` | The key's principal, scopes, expiry, caps |\n| POST | `/v1/keys/{id}/revoke` | any (own key; `admin` for others) | Revoke a key |\n| PATCH | `/v1/keys/{id}` | any (lower); `admin` (raise) | Set a key's hard caps |\n| GET | `/v1/wallet` | `read` | Balance, reserved, spendable, caps, this day's / month's spend |\n| POST | `/v1/wallet/topup` | `write` | Create a Stripe Checkout session (card rail) |\n| POST | `/v1/stripe/webhook` | Stripe signature | Credit the wallet when a Checkout completes (idempotent by event id) |\n| POST | `/v1/quote` | `read` | The exact cost of a call, nothing reserved |\n| GET | `/v1/receipts?since=&limit=` | `read` | Per-call receipts, oldest first |\n| POST | `/v1/receipts/{id}/dispute` | `write` | Open a dispute on a receipt (one per receipt) |\n| ALL | `/mcp` | by unit (see below) | The metered MCP Streamable HTTP proxy |\n\n## 1. Register\n\n```sh\nBASE=https://api.shogunaios.com\nCH=$(curl -s $BASE/v1/agents/challenge)            # {\"challenge\":\"…\",\"difficulty_bits\":18,\"expires_at\":\"…\"}\nCHALLENGE=$(echo \"$CH\" | jq -r .challenge)\nBITS=$(echo \"$CH\" | jq -r .difficulty_bits)\n# Find a nonce such that sha256(challenge + nonce) has $BITS leading zero bits (src/pow.ts `solvePow`\n# does this in a loop; at 18 bits it takes well under a second).\nNONCE=$(node -e '\n  const {createHash}=require(\"node:crypto\");const [c,b]=process.argv.slice(1);\n  const lz=h=>{let n=0;for(const ch of h){const v=parseInt(ch,16);if(v===0){n+=4;continue;}n+=Math.clz32(v)-28;break;}return n;};\n  for(let i=0;;i++){const n=i.toString(36);if(lz(createHash(\"sha256\").update(c+n).digest(\"hex\"))>=Number(b)){console.log(n);break;}}' \"$CHALLENGE\" \"$BITS\")\n\ncurl -s -X POST $BASE/v1/agents \\\n  -H \"X-Agent-Pow: $CHALLENGE:$NONCE\" \\\n  -H 'content-type: application/json' \\\n  -d '{\"ref\":\"dev_alice\"}'\n```\n\n```json\n{\n  \"key\": \"sa_live_1ZqK…(43 chars)\",\n  \"key_id\": \"ak_8c1f…\", \"prefix\": \"1ZqK7v0wQp\",\n  \"principal_id\": \"prn_…\", \"tenant_id\": \"prn_…\",\n  \"scopes\": [\"read\", \"write\", \"run\"],\n  \"created_at\": \"2026-10-25T12:00:00.000Z\", \"expires_at\": \"2027-10-25T12:00:00.000Z\", \"ref\": \"dev_alice\",\n  \"wallet\": { \"balance_micro_usd\": 5000000, \"reserved_micro_usd\": 0 },\n  \"caps\": { \"key\": { \"day_micro_usd\": 1000000, \"month_micro_usd\": 20000000 }, \"principal\": { \"day_micro_usd\": 5000000, \"month_micro_usd\": 100000000 } },\n  \"next_action\": null\n}\n```\n\nBody fields (all optional): `ref` (another key id or a developer id, `[A-Za-z0-9_.:-]{1,128}`),\n`caps: { day_micro_usd, month_micro_usd }` (may only *lower* the key defaults).\n\nRefusals: `401 pow_missing | pow_malformed | pow_invalid | pow_expired | pow_insufficient |\npow_reused` (`next_action: \"solve_challenge\"`), `429 rate_limited` (per IP per hour, with\n`Retry-After`), `400 invalid_ref | invalid_caps | caps_above_default`.\n\n## 2. Call MCP (metered)\n\nThe proxy reads three headers and never the body:\n\n| Header | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `Mcp-Method` | the JSON-RPC method (`tools/call`, `initialize`, `tools/list`, …). Anything but `tools/call` is free `protocol` traffic |\n| `Mcp-Name` | the tool name for `tools/call` |\n| `Mcp-Quantity` | units in this call (events for `memory.ingest`); default `1`, max `1,000,000` |\n| `Mcp-Dry-Run: true` | quote only: nothing reserved, nothing forwarded |\n| `Mcp-Tool-Id` | for `router.run`: the tool whose provider price the router looks up |\n\nClassification (`src/pricing.ts`):\n\n| Unit | Price | Scope needed | Tools |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `read` | $0 | `read` | `state.*`, `memory.search`, `memory.recall`, `memory.get_context_pack`, `memory.get_context`, `memory.get_events`, `memory.read_session`, `memory.get_wrap`, `memory.get_periods`, `calendar.upcoming`, `profile.whoami`, `wiki.evidence`, `actions.status`, `approvals.*`, `router.discover`, `router.inspect` |\n| `ingest` | 500 µ$ per event ($0.50 / 1,000) | `write` | `memory.ingest`, `memory.append_note` |\n| `model_op` | 100 µ$ per call ($0.10 / 1,000) | `write` | every other `tools/call` |\n| `run` | provider price × 1.05, rounded up | `run` | `router.run`, `actions.execute` (price from the router; from headers alone a run is refused `400 run_price_unavailable`) |\n| `storage` | 100,000 µ$ per GB-month | — | booked by the storage sweep, not per call |\n| `protocol` | $0 | any valid key | every non-`tools/call` method and `GET`/`DELETE /mcp` |\n\n```sh\ncurl -s -X POST $BASE/mcp \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer $KEY\" -H 'content-type: application/json' \\\n  -H 'Mcp-Method: tools/call' -H 'Mcp-Name: memory.ingest' -H 'Mcp-Quantity: 12' \\\n  -d '{\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"id\":1,\"method\":\"tools/call\",\"params\":{\"name\":\"memory.ingest\",\"arguments\":{…}}}' -i\n```\n\n```\nHTTP/1.1 200 OK\nX-Receipt-Id: rcpt_…\nX-Usage-Unit: ingest\nX-Usage-Quantity: 12\nX-Usage-Cost-Micro-Usd: 6000\nX-Latency-Ms: 41\n```\n\nThe body is the core's, relayed untouched. Sequence inside the gateway: verify key → classify →\nquote → **reserve** (wallet and the four cap counters in one transaction) → forward → **settle**\nonce (2xx: the quote; anything else: 0, so a failed call costs nothing). A free call writes a\nsettled receipt at cost 0.\n\nA dry run answers `200 { \"dry_run\": true, \"quote\": { \"unit\", \"quantity\", \"cost_micro_usd\" },\n\"sufficient\": true, \"balance_micro_usd\": …, \"usage\": {…,\"cost_micro_usd\":0}, \"latency_ms\" }`.\nThe real call then settles at exactly `quote.cost_micro_usd`.\n\nRefusals: `401` (key), `403 insufficient_scope`, `402 payment_required | cap_exceeded`\n(`docs/api/402.md`), `503 upstream_unavailable | wallet_unavailable | capacity_exhausted`\n(the company's global daily breaker; `Retry-After` to UTC midnight).\n\n## 3. Quote\n\n```sh\ncurl -s -X POST $BASE/v1/quote -H \"Authorization: Bearer $KEY\" -H 'content-type: application/json' \\\n  -d '{\"method\":\"tools/call\",\"name\":\"memory.ingest\",\"quantity\":250}'\n```\n\n```json\n{ \"quote\": { \"unit\": \"ingest\", \"quantity\": 250, \"cost_micro_usd\": 125000 }, \"method\": \"tools/call\", \"name\": \"memory.ingest\",\n  \"balance_micro_usd\": 5000000, \"sufficient\": true, \"caps\": { \"…\" }, \"usage\": { \"unit\": \"ingest\", \"quantity\": 0, \"cost_micro_usd\": 0 }, \"next_action\": null }\n```\n\nFor a run pass `provider_price_micro_usd` (from `router.inspect`): `{\"name\":\"router.run\",\"provider_price_micro_usd\":1999}` → `2099`.\n\n## 4. Wallet and top-up\n\n```sh\ncurl -s $BASE/v1/wallet -H \"Authorization: Bearer $KEY\"\n```\n\n```json\n{ \"principal_id\": \"prn_…\", \"balance_micro_usd\": 4994000, \"reserved_micro_usd\": 0, \"available_micro_usd\": 4994000,\n  \"caps\": { \"…\" }, \"spend\": { \"key\": { \"day_micro_usd\": 6000, \"month_micro_usd\": 6000 }, \"principal\": { \"day_micro_usd\": 6000, \"month_micro_usd\": 6000 } },\n  \"topup\": { \"rail\": \"card\", \"url\": \"https://api.shogunaios.com/v1/wallet/topup\", \"method\": \"POST\", \"min_micro_usd\": 5000000, \"max_micro_usd\": 500000000 } }\n```\n\n```sh\ncurl -s -X POST $BASE/v1/wallet/topup -H \"Authorization: Bearer $KEY\" -H 'content-type: application/json' \\\n  -H 'Idempotency-Key: topup-2026-10-25-1' -d '{\"amount_micro_usd\":10000000}'\n```\n\n```json\n{ \"rail\": \"card\", \"url\": \"https://checkout.stripe.com/c/pay/cs_…\", \"session_id\": \"cs_…\", \"amount_micro_usd\": 10000000,\n  \"next_action\": \"approve_at_url\", \"instructions\": \"Open url in a browser and pay. …\" }\n```\n\nHand `url` to a human once. Stripe calls `POST /v1/stripe/webhook` (configure the endpoint for\n`checkout.session.completed`; `Stripe-Signature` is verified with `STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET`); the\nwallet is credited `amount_total` once per event id. Then retry the `402`'d call unchanged.\nWithout `STRIPE_SECRET_KEY` the route answers `503 topup_unavailable`.\n\n## 5. Caps\n\nDefaults (`src/caps.ts`): key $1/day, $20/month; principal $5/day, $100/month. Checked at\nreservation against booked estimates (so in-flight calls count), corrected at settlement.\nUTC periods.\n\n```sh\ncurl -s -X PATCH $BASE/v1/keys/$KEY_ID -H \"Authorization: Bearer $KEY\" -H 'content-type: application/json' \\\n  -d '{\"day_micro_usd\":250000}'                       # lower: any key; raise above default: admin only\ncurl -s -X POST  $BASE/v1/keys/$KEY_ID/revoke -H \"Authorization: Bearer $KEY\"\n```\n\nExceeding a cap: `402 cap_exceeded`, `payment.cap = { scope, period, limit_micro_usd,\nspent_micro_usd }`, `Retry-After` until the period resets. Deployment-wide defaults:\n`AGENT_KEY_CAP_DAY_USD` and friends.\n\n## 6. Receipts and disputes\n\n```sh\ncurl -s \"$BASE/v1/receipts?since=2026-10-25T00:00:00Z&limit=100\" -H \"Authorization: Bearer $KEY\"\n```\n\n```json\n{ \"receipts\": [\n    { \"id\": \"rcpt_…\", \"key_id\": \"ak_…\", \"method\": \"tools/call\", \"name\": \"memory.ingest\", \"unit\": \"ingest\", \"quantity\": 12,\n      \"cost_micro_usd\": 6000, \"reserved_micro_usd\": null, \"settled\": true, \"ts\": \"2026-10-25T12:00:05.000Z\" } ],\n  \"next_since\": \"2026-10-25T12:00:05.001Z\" }\n```\n\n`since` is ISO-8601 or epoch milliseconds; page with `next_since`. `reserved_micro_usd` is\nnon-null only while a call is in flight.\n\n```sh\ncurl -s -X POST $BASE/v1/receipts/rcpt_…/dispute -H \"Authorization: Bearer $KEY\" -H 'content-type: application/json' \\\n  -d '{\"reason\":\"ingest counted 12 events, I sent 10\"}'\n```\n\n`201 { \"dispute_id\": \"dsp_…\", \"receipt_id\": \"rcpt_…\", \"status\": \"open\", \"ts\": \"…\" }`; a second\ndispute on the same receipt answers `200` with the existing one. Someone else's receipt is `404`.\n\n## Licence tokens and agent keys together (E3-T4)\n\nThe Ed25519 licence token stays the credential of the Mac app's routes. Both credentials meter\ninto the same string space (`auth.ts` `meterSubjectOf`: a licence id or `account:<acc>` for a\nlicence, `principal:<id>` for an agent key), and cost-bearing agent calls book their quote\nagainst the same global daily breaker row the Batch lane uses (`relay_spend_days`), so the\ncompany's spend ceiling covers both. The wallet, caps and receipts are the agent side's own\ntables.\n\n## Operations\n\n- Provision: `psql … -f apps/api/sql/agent-keys.sql` then `-f apps/api/sql/agent-keys-permissions.sql`.\n- Secrets (`wrangler secret put`): `AGENT_POW_SECRET`, `STRIPE_SECRET_KEY`, `STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET`.\n- Vars: `AGENT_API_ENABLED=1`, `PUBLIC_BASE_URL`, optionally `AGENT_POW_BITS`,\n  `AGENT_REGISTER_PER_HOUR`, `AGENT_KEY_PBKDF2_ITERATIONS`, the four `AGENT_*_CAP_*_USD`.\n- Boot log: `agent_api open (card top-ups on)` / `agent_api closed (AGENT_API_ENABLED unset)`.\n- Log events to alert on: `event=payment_required` runs (a client looping on 402),\n  `event=capacity_exhausted` (the breaker closed the day), `event=webhook_rejected` (a bad\n  Stripe signature), `stripe_failure status=…`.\n- The periodic sweep deletes expired PoW rows and settled receipts older than 400 days;\n  unsettled receipts are never deleted (they hold money).\n\nNot in this slice: the core behind `/mcp` (E4 wires `mcpUpstream`; until then `/mcp` answers\n`503 upstream_unavailable` before reserving), the router's `runPricer`, storage billing (the\nrate is published; the sweep that books it comes with the core's usage report), and OAuth/CIMD\n(E3-T2).\n\n---\n\n<!-- file: docs/api/402.md -->\n\n# 402 Payment Required\n\nThe gateway answers `402` whenever a metered call cannot proceed for a money reason. There are\ntwo reasons and one shape. Implementation: `apps/api/src/billing/402.ts`; tests:\n`apps/api/test/metering.test.ts`. The plan's wording is `docs/implementation-plan.md` §7.3.\n\n## When\n\n| `error.code` | Meaning | `next_action` | `payment.accepts` | `retry_after` |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `payment_required` | The wallet's spendable balance (`balance − reserved`) is below the quoted cost. | `top_up` | `[{ rail: \"card\", url, method: \"POST\" }]` | `0` |\n| `cap_exceeded` | A hard cap (per key or per principal, daily or monthly) would be crossed. Money does not help. | `retry_after` | `[]` | seconds until the period resets (UTC midnight, or the first of next month) |\n\nEvery metered route returns this shape: the `/mcp` proxy (classified from `Mcp-Method` /\n`Mcp-Name`), and any REST route mounted with the same metering middleware. Free calls (`read`,\n`protocol`) never 402 — a wallet at zero still reads its own state.\n\nNothing is reserved, forwarded or recorded on a 402. The call is a no-op; retrying it after the\nremedy is safe.\n\n## Body\n\n```json\n{\n  \"error\": {\n    \"code\": \"payment_required\",\n    \"message\": \"This call costs 6000 micro-USD and the wallet has 1000 spendable. Top up at least 5000 micro-USD, then retry the same request.\",\n    \"next_action\": \"top_up\"\n  },\n  \"payment\": {\n    \"amount_micro_usd\": 6000,\n    \"unit\": \"ingest\",\n    \"accepts\": [{ \"rail\": \"card\", \"url\": \"https://api.shogunaios.com/v1/wallet/topup\", \"method\": \"POST\" }],\n    \"retry_after\": 0,\n    \"balance_micro_usd\": 1000\n  },\n  \"usage\": { \"unit\": \"ingest\", \"quantity\": 0, \"cost_micro_usd\": 0 },\n  \"latency_ms\": 3\n}\n```\n\nFor `cap_exceeded`, `payment.balance_micro_usd` is replaced by `payment.cap`:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"error\": { \"code\": \"cap_exceeded\", \"message\": \"…\", \"next_action\": \"retry_after\" },\n  \"payment\": {\n    \"amount_micro_usd\": 500,\n    \"unit\": \"ingest\",\n    \"accepts\": [],\n    \"retry_after\": 43200,\n    \"cap\": { \"scope\": \"key\", \"period\": \"day\", \"limit_micro_usd\": 1000000, \"spent_micro_usd\": 999600 }\n  },\n  \"usage\": { \"unit\": \"ingest\", \"quantity\": 0, \"cost_micro_usd\": 0 },\n  \"latency_ms\": 2\n}\n```\n\nField notes:\n\n- Amounts are integer micro-USD (1 USD = 1,000,000). `amount_micro_usd` is the exact figure the\n  call would have reserved — the same number `POST /v1/quote` and `Mcp-Dry-Run: true` return.\n- `unit` is one of `ingest`, `model_op`, `run`, `storage` (`/.well-known/pricing.json`).\n- `usage` and `latency_ms` are present so the envelope matches a `200`'s (§7.2); the quantity is\n  `0` because nothing was billed.\n- `accepts` is ordered by preference. Today it holds the `card` rail; an `x402` rail can be\n  appended later without changing the shape.\n\n## Headers\n\n| Header | Value |\n|---|---|\n| `PAYMENT-REQUIRED` | base64url of the x402-style JSON below |\n| `Retry-After` | present only for `cap_exceeded`, equal to `payment.retry_after` |\n| `Cache-Control` | `no-store` |\n| `Content-Type` | `application/json` |\n\nThe header payload (decoded):\n\n```json\n{\n  \"x402Version\": 2,\n  \"error\": \"payment_required\",\n  \"accepts\": [{ \"rail\": \"card\", \"url\": \"https://api.shogunaios.com/v1/wallet/topup\", \"method\": \"POST\" }],\n  \"next_action\": \"top_up\",\n  \"payment\": { \"amount_micro_usd\": 6000, \"unit\": \"ingest\", \"accepts\": [ \"…\" ], \"retry_after\": 0, \"balance_micro_usd\": 1000 }\n}\n```\n\n`x402Version`, a string `error` and `accepts[]` are the fields x402's `payment-required`\nconvention puts at the top level; `payment` and `next_action` carry the plan's fields so a client\nthat reads only headers has the full quote.\n\n## The card rail\n\n1. The agent calls `POST /v1/wallet/topup` with its key and `{\"amount_micro_usd\": 10000000}`\n   ($10; min $5, max $500). The answer is `201` with `url` (a Stripe Checkout page) and\n   `next_action: \"approve_at_url\"`.\n2. A human opens the URL once and pays (URL elicitation). The agent does not need to poll\n   Stripe: the gateway's webhook (`POST /v1/stripe/webhook`) credits the wallet when Stripe\n   confirms, idempotently by event id.\n3. The agent retries the call that answered `402`, unchanged.\n\n## Client guidance\n\n- Branch on `error.code`, then on `next_action`. Do not parse `message`.\n- On `payment_required`, compare `payment.amount_micro_usd` with `payment.balance_micro_usd` to\n  size the top-up; the minimum top-up is `$5`.\n- On `cap_exceeded`, sleep `retry_after` seconds or send less. A key may lower its own caps\n  (`PATCH /v1/keys/{id}`); raising them above the defaults needs `admin`.\n- A `402` is never logged with the request body on the gateway side, and never includes it.\n\n---\n\n<!-- file: docs/api/agent-surfaces.md -->\n\n# Agent-readable surfaces\n\nEvery place an agent looks before it chooses a service, and what generates it (CLAUDE.md\ninvariant 12: these are derived from code and config; the human docs derive from them). Plan: E10.\n\n| Surface | URL / file | Generated by | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| MCP Server Card | `GET /.well-known/mcp/server-card.json` | `apps/api/src/wellknown/server-card.ts` | the tool table (`apps/api/fixtures/tool-table.json`, [tool-table.json.md](tool-table.json.md)) + `identity.ts` |\n| MCP Registry entry | `server.json` (repository root) | hand-written, validated by `pnpm registry:validate` against the registry schema and pinned to `identity.ts` / `packages/cli` by tests | — |\n| A2A Agent Card | `GET /.well-known/agent-card.json` | `agent-card.ts`; skills from `scripts/gen-agent-skills.mjs` (`agent-skills.generated.json`) | `SKILL.md` \"When to use this skill\"; signed with `AGENT_CARD_SIGNING_KEY` (Ed25519, detached JWS over RFC 8785) when set |\n| Pricing | `GET /.well-known/pricing.json` | `apps/api/src/pricing.ts` `pricingDocument` | the metering table (`RATE_MICROS`, `RUN_MARKUP_BPS`, `FREE_ALLOWANCE_MICROS`, caps) |\n| Status / health | `GET /.well-known/status.json`, `GET /health` | `status.ts` | a five-minute in-process window over the agent routes + `apps/api/incidents.json` |\n| OpenAPI 3.1 | `GET /openapi.json` | `openapi.ts` | the route table in code; the core's REST routes join when E4 exports `rest_routing.rs` |\n| llms.txt | `llms.txt`, `llms-full.txt` (root); `GET /llms.txt`, `GET /llms-full.txt` | `scripts/gen-llms-txt.mjs` (`--check` in CI) | README.md, SKILL.md, apps/api/README-agent-api.md, docs/api/*.md |\n| Smithery | `smithery.yaml` (root), `GET /.well-known/mcp-config` | `mcp-config.ts`; the YAML mirrors it and `identity.ts` | — |\n| Claude Code plugin | `.claude-plugin/marketplace.json`, `.claude-plugin/plugin.json` | hand-written, pinned to `identity.ts` by tests | hosted MCP server + `npx shogunagent@0.1 mcp`; the root `SKILL.md` is the skill |\n| CLI | `npx shogunagent` (`packages/cli`) | — | `shogunagent help --json` names commands, endpoints and the skill version |\n| Skill | `SKILL.md` (root), `https://shogunagent.dev/SKILL.md` | hand-written (E10-T5); the selection eval (E11) gates edits | — |\n| Benchmarks | `benchmarks.json` | E10-T8, from `crates/memory-bench` (E12) | not in this slice |\n\n## What is fixture-driven until E4 lands\n\nE4 rewrites the Rust tool catalog (`crates/shogun-mcp/src/tool_catalog.rs`: descriptions,\nannotations, the core 22 freeze). Until it does:\n\n- `apps/api/fixtures/tool-table.json` is maintained by hand to the plan's §7.1 list and marked\n  `\"source\": \"fixture\"`. A test asserts its names equal §7.1 and its cost units equal\n  `pricing.ts`. E4 adds the export (`shogun-mcp --export-tool-table`, name to be confirmed), CI\n  regenerates the fixture from it, and the `source` field changes.\n- The server card, the OpenAPI `x-mcp.tools` block and the CLI's read-only set\n  (`packages/cli/src/readonly-proxy.ts`, pinned to the fixture by a test) all follow the fixture.\n- The OpenAPI document covers the routes `apps/api` and `apps/router` serve today. The core's\n  `/v1/state/*`, `/v1/memory/*`, `/v1/actions/*`, `/v1/export` routes are added when E4 exports\n  `rest_routing.rs` the same way.\n\n## Identity\n\nOne module, `apps/api/src/wellknown/identity.ts`, holds the name (`io.github.torutesu/shogunagent`),\ndisplay name, version (`0.1.0`, equal to `SKILL.md` and the `shogunagent` npm package), short and\nlong descriptions, provider and URLs. `apps/api/test/wellknown.test.ts` pins `server.json`,\n`.claude-plugin/*.json`, `smithery.yaml` and `SKILL.md`'s version to it.\n\n## Deployment settings (apps/api)\n\n- `AGENT_CARD_SIGNING_KEY` — optional Ed25519 private key (PKCS#8 PEM, or the 32-byte seed as\n  hex / base64). Unset: the card is served without a `signatures` field. A value that is not an\n  Ed25519 key stops boot.\n- `AGENT_MCP_URL` — the MCP endpoint the documents name when it is not `<PUBLIC_BASE_URL>/mcp`.\n- `apps/api/incidents.json` — the public incident history: `[{ id: \"inc_<slug>\", title, severity:\n  \"minor\" | \"major\", started_at, resolved_at | null, affected: [route templates], updates: [{ at,\n  text }] }]`. An open `major` incident makes `/health` answer 503.\n\n---\n\n<!-- file: docs/api/core-tools.md -->\n\n# Core tools (frozen wire names)\n\n<!-- Generated by `shogun_mcp::core_tools::render_core_tools_markdown`; `cargo test -p shogun-mcp --test wire_freeze` fails when this file is edited by hand. -->\n\nThe core set is the 21 tools every ShogunAgent face lists under `SHOGUN_MCP_TOOLSETS=core`, in this order. Their wire names, input property names and annotations are frozen (CLAUDE.md invariant 11): a change is a new name plus a note in `docs/api/deprecation-policy.md`, never an edit in place. Annotations are generated from the level (`shogun_mcp::core_tools::annotations`); the cost class is what the gateway meters from `Mcp-Method` / `Mcp-Name` (reads are free, invariant 10).\n\n| # | Tool | Level | Cost | Annotations | Input properties | Description |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | `memory.search` | read | free | readOnlyHint, idempotentHint | depth, from_ms, limit, mode, query, source_kind, to_ms | Find where something appears in the user's memory (screen text, mail, meetings, notes, people, projects, summaries) by keyword or topic. Returns ranked hits with source ids and confidence: an index, not evidence; open hits with memory.get_events before quoting. Not for \"what is true now\" (memory.recall) and not the web. Narrow with a distinctive query, from_ms/to_ms (unix ms) and source_kind; limit 1-50 (default 20); depth all needs both bounds. Free. |\n| 2 | `memory.get_context` | read | free | readOnlyHint, idempotentHint | — | What the user is doing right now on their device: the current app/window session with its latest captured text, the recent sessions, key facts and notes. Empty when no capture source feeds this store. Not a search (memory.recall) and not an earlier span (memory.read_session). Free. |\n| 3 | `memory.get_context_pack` | read | free | readOnlyHint, idempotentHint | query | One grounded pack for a person, project or task: confidence-gated facts plus dated, attributed evidence lines with event ids, the same assembly the in-app chat uses. Moderate size; prefer memory.recall when you have a token budget. Not the current screen (memory.get_context). Free. |\n| 4 | `memory.get_events` | read | free | readOnlyHint, idempotentHint | ids, max_chars | Open the source text behind a search hit, a pack line or recall evidence: up to 20 event ids, each with time, app/window, source and its stored text (max_chars each, default 8000). Unknown ids come back under missing. Free. |\n| 5 | `memory.read_session` | read | free | readOnlyHint, idempotentHint | app, from_ms, limit, to_ms | What happened over a span of at most 12 hours: captured rows merged into app/window sessions with start, end, duration, latest text and event ids to open. Optional app filter (name or bundle id). Not a summary (memory.get_periods). Free. |\n| 6 | `memory.get_wrap` | read | free | readOnlyHint, idempotentHint | — | Today's wrap-up as the user sees it: outcome counts, still-open items, tomorrow's first items and loose ends. Small. For the narrative of the day use memory.get_periods. Free. |\n| 7 | `memory.get_periods` | read | free | readOnlyHint, idempotentHint | from_ms, page_size, to_ms | Stored hour/day/week/month summaries of a time window (from_ms/to_ms, unix ms; default the last 24 h): use for \"today\", \"yesterday\", \"this week\", \"between 2 and 4pm\". Summaries are an index: open sources with memory.search or memory.get_events before quoting. Up to page_size periods (default 24, max 48). Free. |\n| 8 | `memory.recall` | read | free | readOnlyHint, idempotentHint | budget_tokens, include_scratch, query | What memory holds on a question, cut to budget_tokens: High-confidence facts as facts, Medium ones prefixed \"possibly:\", then dated evidence with event ids until the budget is spent. The right first call before acting on the user's work. Reads only this principal's store, never the web; include_scratch adds your own scratch namespace. Free. |\n| 9 | `calendar.upcoming` | read | free | readOnlyHint, idempotentHint | page_size | The user's upcoming calendar from now through the next 7 days (default 10 events, page_size up to 100): title, start/end (unix ms), all_day, location, attendee count, local day. Calendar facts, never hedged. Empty when no calendar is connected. Free. |\n| 10 | `profile.whoami` | read | free | readOnlyHint, idempotentHint | — | Who the user is: identity (name, email, role, tools, habits, style; read first), the declared profile, the About You projection and a work summary, with guesses under work.inferred. Free. |\n| 11 | `state.commitments.list` | read | free | readOnlyHint, idempotentHint | include_low, limit, offset | What the user committed to: open commitments with description, due date, status, confidence and the quote that proves each one. Rows marked possibly are Medium confidence; say so, never act on them externally. Newest first; limit/offset. Free. |\n| 12 | `state.open_loops.list` | read | free | readOnlyHint, idempotentHint | include_low, limit, offset | What the user is waiting on or has left unresolved: open loops with description, status, confidence and the proving quote. Rows marked possibly are Medium confidence. Newest first; limit/offset. Free. |\n| 13 | `state.people.list` | read | free | readOnlyHint, idempotentHint | include_low, limit, offset | The people the user works with, ranked by engagement: id, display name, confidence. Follow with state.people.get for one person's evidence sources. Free. |\n| 14 | `state.projects.list` | read | free | readOnlyHint, idempotentHint | include_low, limit, offset | The projects the user is on, ranked by engagement: id, name, confidence. Follow with state.projects.get for one project's evidence sources. Free. |\n| 15 | `state.people.get` | read | free | readOnlyHint, idempotentHint | id | One person by id: display name, confidence and where the evidence comes from (sources and counts, never content). not_found for an unknown id. Free. |\n| 16 | `state.projects.get` | read | free | readOnlyHint, idempotentHint | id | One project by id: name, confidence and where the evidence comes from (sources and counts, never content). not_found for an unknown id. Free. |\n| 17 | `wiki.evidence` | read | free | readOnlyHint, idempotentHint | query | The source quotes behind a person or project page (query: the name or key): dated excerpts with event ids. Use before quoting a fact from a page or a pack. Free. |\n| 18 | `actions.status` | read | free | readOnlyHint, idempotentHint | approval_id | Poll one L3 approval by approval_id: pending, sent, rejected, timed_out, send_failed, draft_saved or outcome_unknown. Never claim an action happened before this answers sent. Free. |\n| 19 | `memory.append_note` | write L1 | ingest | — | text | Append one note worth remembering (text, at most 2000 characters): a durable L1 write to the user's own memory, deduplicated, nothing sent anywhere. Not for conversation transcripts or batches (memory.ingest). Metered per event (ingest). |\n| 20 | `memory.ingest` | write L1 | ingest | — | actor, events, idempotency_key, metadata, namespace, source, thread_key, turns | Hand over what you learned so the next agent starts from it: events[] (ts_ms, kind, title, content) or turns[] (role, content) under a source, an actor and a thread_key, with a required idempotency_key; the same key replays the first result and adds nothing. Text is sanitized, secrets are masked, exact duplicates collapse, then the rows join the event log for the hourly review that updates state. Local L1 write; nothing leaves the device. Not for verbatim transcripts of whole sessions: ingest the turns that changed something. Metered per event (ingest). |\n| 21 | `actions.execute` | per action (L1 / L3 by kind) | run | destructiveHint, openWorldHint | body, bundle_id, channel, kind, latest_message_id, latest_reply_ts, path, query, subject, target, text, thread_id, thread_ts, to | Run an action for the user. kind local_search runs now (L1) and returns results; open_app, reveal_file, show_notification and copy_to_clipboard act on the user's Mac and run only from the ShogunAI app (refused here, nothing done); send_email, post_message and post_comment never run here: they queue for the user's explicit approval (L3) and answer resultType approval_required with an approval URL and approval_id to poll with actions.status. Never report a send as done before actions.status says sent. Metered as a run. |\n\nDeprecated core tools: none.\n\n---\n\n<!-- file: docs/api/deprecation-policy.md -->\n\n# Deprecation policy for the agent-facing API\n\nApplies to every surface an agent reads or calls: the core tool set (`docs/api/core-tools.md`),\nevery other MCP tool name, the REST paths and the CLI subcommands that mirror them, the result\nenvelopes (`result`, `usage`, `latency_ms`, `next_action`), the MRTR / approval shapes\n(`resultType`, `inputRequests`, `approval`), and the generated files (`server-card.json`,\n`openapi.json`, `pricing.json`, `llms.txt`).\n\n## Rules\n\n1. **Wire names are frozen** (CLAUDE.md invariant 11). A core tool's name, the names of its input\n   properties and its annotations never change in place. `tests/wire_freeze.rs` fails on any\n   edit; a change ships as a **new name** plus a deprecation of the old one.\n2. **Additive changes need no deprecation.** A new optional input property, a new field in a\n   result, a new tool or a new group may land at any time. Agents must ignore fields they do not\n   know.\n3. **A deprecated tool keeps working for 12 months** from the release that deprecates it (the\n   MCP 2026-07-28 window). During the window it answers exactly as before. It is removed in the\n   first release after the window, and its name is never reused.\n4. **A deprecation is visible on the wire.** The tool's `tools/list` descriptor carries a\n   `deprecated` string (`shogun_mcp::memory_api::tool_deprecation`) of the form\n   `\"<since: YYYY-MM-DD>: use <replacement>; removed after <YYYY-MM-DD>\"`. The description keeps\n   its first sentence so an agent that already selected the tool still understands what it did.\n   `docs/api/core-tools.md` lists deprecated core tools in its last line (generated).\n5. **The replacement exists before the deprecation.** A deprecation note names a tool that is\n   already listed, so an agent can switch in the same session.\n6. **Semantics never change under a name.** When what a tool does changes (its level, its cost\n   class, what it reads or writes), that is a new tool. A bug fix that makes a tool do what its\n   description already said is not a change.\n7. **Levels only tighten in place.** A tool may move from L1 to L2 or L3 (more confirmation)\n   without a new name, because an agent that treated it as L1 is told by the MRTR / approval\n   result and nothing runs without the confirmation. A tool never loosens in place: L3 → L1 is a\n   new name.\n8. **Error codes are additive.** A new `error.code` or `next_action` value may appear; an existing\n   one keeps its meaning. Agents treat an unknown `next_action` as \"stop and tell the human\".\n9. **REST and CLI follow the tool.** The REST path and CLI subcommand of a deprecated tool are\n   deprecated and removed on the same dates; the CLI prints the note to stderr once per run.\n10. **Generated surfaces regenerate.** `server-card.json`, `openapi.json` and `llms.txt` are built\n    from the tool table, so a deprecation appears in all of them in the same release; none is\n    edited by hand (CLAUDE.md invariant 12).\n\n## How to deprecate a tool\n\n1. Add the replacement tool (new `Tool` variant, wire name, level, group, description, REST path,\n   CLI subcommand), with its tests.\n2. Add a row to `DEPRECATED_TOOLS` in `crates/shogun-mcp/src/memory_api.rs`:\n   `(Tool::Old, \"2027-01-15: use new.tool; removed after 2028-01-15\")`.\n3. Run `WIRE_FREEZE_UPDATE=1 cargo test -p shogun-mcp --test wire_freeze` to regenerate the\n   snapshot and `docs/api/core-tools.md`, and review the diff: only the `deprecated` field and the\n   new tool may appear.\n4. Note the deprecation in the release notes under \"Deprecated\", with the removal date.\n5. After the removal date: remove the variant, the row, the REST path and the CLI subcommand in\n   one commit; regenerate the snapshot; the name stays retired.\n\n## Not covered\n\n- `SHOGUN_MCP_TOOLSETS` group ids are a listing convenience, not a wire contract; a group may be\n  renamed with one release of overlap.\n- The handshake `instructions` text and tool descriptions are prose: they change freely, subject\n  to the selection eval (E11) not regressing.\n- Internal error messages after the `code:` prefix.\n\n---\n\n<!-- file: docs/api/export-v1.md -->\n\n# Export format v1\n\nThe portable form of one principal's world model: people, projects, commitments and open loops,\nwith the confidence, the validity interval, the writer, the namespace and the quote that proves\neach one, plus the rows that were deleted. It is how a user takes their state to another store\n(or another product) and how a sync peer keeps up. The JSON Schema for every line is\n[`export-v1.schema.json`](export-v1.schema.json); the implementation is\n`crates/shogun-memory/src/export/` and a test validates every exported line against the schema.\n\n## Getting one\n\n| Face | Call |\n|---|---|\n| MCP | `state.export` with `{\"format\": \"export-v1\"}`, plus `\"differential\": true` or `\"since_cursor\": <ms>` |\n| REST | `POST /v1/export` with the same body (`format` defaults to `export-v1`); `GET /v1/state/export` stays the Markdown export |\n| CLI | `shogun state export --format export-v1 [--differential \\| --since-cursor <ms>]` |\n\nThe result is the usual envelope: `result.jsonl` (the export), `result.cursor`,\n`result.since_cursor`, `result.records`, a free `usage` (exports are reads, invariant 10) and\n`latency_ms`.\n\n- **Full** (no option): every row of the four state tables, in every namespace and every\n  confidence band — the export belongs to the user — and every tombstone.\n- **`since_cursor: <ms>`**: only rows changed after that time (`updated_at > since`) and the\n  tombstones written after it. The stored cursor is not touched.\n- **`differential: true`**: `since_cursor` taken from the store's own `export_cursor`\n  (`principal_meta`, V96), then advanced to this export's `cursor` in the same transaction. The\n  first differential export is a full one. Advancing the cursor is the only write and holds no\n  content.\n\n## Shape\n\nJSON Lines, UTF-8, one compact object per line. The **first line is the manifest**; the rest are\nrecords in this order: entities, facts, tombstones.\n\n### `manifest`\n\n```json\n{\"type\":\"manifest\",\"format\":\"shogunagent-export/v1\",\"schema_version\":100,\"generated_at\":1792000000000,\n \"since_cursor\":null,\"cursor\":1792000000000,\"counts\":{\"entities\":2,\"facts\":3,\"tombstones\":1}}\n```\n\n`schema_version` is the store's migration version. `cursor` is the export's time, or the newest\nchange it carries when that is later; pass it back as `since_cursor` to continue.\n\n### `entity` — a person or a project\n\n```json\n{\"type\":\"entity\",\"kind\":\"person\",\"id\":12,\"name\":\"Oren Vale\",\"aliases\":[\"Oren\"],\n \"summary\":\"Leads the Heron rollout.\",\"status\":null,\"confidence\":0.9,\"band\":\"high\",\n \"valid_from\":1791990000000,\"valid_to\":null,\"updated_at\":1791995000000,\n \"actor\":\"agent:claude-code\",\"namespace\":\"world\",\n \"provenance\":[{\"event_id\":481,\"quote\":\"Oren Vale: I'll send the rollout plan by Friday.\"}]}\n```\n\n`status` is the project status (`active`, `waiting`, `paused`, `done`) and `null` for a person.\n`valid_to` is set when a project is `done`.\n\n### `fact` — a commitment or an open loop\n\n```json\n{\"type\":\"fact\",\"kind\":\"commitment\",\"id\":7,\"subtype\":\"theirs\",\"text\":\"send the rollout plan by Friday\",\n \"status\":\"open\",\"due_at\":1792300000000,\"counterparty_id\":12,\"project_id\":null,\n \"confidence\":0.9,\"band\":\"high\",\"valid_from\":1791990000000,\"valid_to\":null,\n \"updated_at\":1791995000001,\"actor\":\"system:loop_consolidator\",\"namespace\":\"world\",\n \"provenance\":[{\"event_id\":481,\"quote\":\"I'll send the rollout plan by Friday.\"}]}\n```\n\n`subtype` is the commitment direction (`mine`, `theirs`) or the open-loop kind\n(`reply_needed`, `waiting_on_them`, `review_pending`, `decision_pending`, `follow_up`, `other`).\n`valid_to` is set when the fact stopped being current (`done`, `cancelled`, `closed`).\n`counterparty_id` / `project_id` name entity ids of the same export.\n\n### `tombstone` — a deleted row\n\n```json\n{\"type\":\"tombstone\",\"kind\":\"commitments\",\"id\":9,\"deleted_at\":1791999000000,\"reason\":\"deleted_by:human:owner\"}\n```\n\nThe deleted content is never kept or exported. A consumer that holds a copy of `(kind, id)`\nretires it.\n\n## Fields every record shares\n\n| Field | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `confidence` | The stored confidence, 0–1. |\n| `band` | `high` (≥ 0.8, a fact), `medium` (≥ 0.5, render as `possibly:`), `low` (never render as fact). Same bands every face applies. |\n| `valid_from` / `valid_to` | When the row became current and when it stopped (`null` while current), unix ms. |\n| `updated_at` | The last change, unix ms; what a differential export filters on. |\n| `actor` | The last writer (`human:owner`, `agent:<id>`, `connector:<source>`, `system:<job>`); `null` for rows written before V93. |\n| `namespace` | `world` or `agent_scratch:<agent_id>`. |\n| `provenance` | `{event_id, quote}` pointers: the event in the exporting store and the exact span of it that proves the row (`quote` is `null` when none was retained). Event bodies are not exported: the export is the world model, not the log. |\n\n## Importing one\n\n`memory.import` (MCP), `POST /v1/memory/import` (REST) and `shogun memory import` (CLI) accept an\nexport v1 in the `package` slot, as its JSON Lines text. Preview reports what it holds\n(`\"action\": \"restore\"` and the counts); commit restores it, no `select` needed:\n\n- every entity and fact becomes a new row of this store, with new ids; facts keep pointing at the\n  entities they name;\n- each provenance pointer becomes one event of its own (source `import`) whose text is the quote,\n  or the row's text when there was no quote, so every row keeps its provenance and its quote;\n- confidence is capped at 0.6 (Medium): an imported claim is not something this store observed,\n  the same rule as every other import, until the review confirms it;\n- the importer is the writer (`actor`) of every restored row;\n- tombstones name ids of the exporting store; they are counted and not applied.\n\nExport → import into a fresh store gives the same row counts and the same quotes (tested).\n\n---\n\n<!-- file: docs/api/tool-table.json.md -->\n\n# `tool-table.json` — the machine-readable tool table\n\nThe one input every agent-readable surface is rendered from (CLAUDE.md invariant 12):\n`/.well-known/mcp/server-card.json` (E10-T1), the MCP `tools/list` the core answers, the\n`llms.txt` tool index, the read-only filter of `npx shogunagent mcp` (E10-T10), and the OpenAPI\ndocument's tool-call section (E10-T6).\n\n## Where it comes from\n\nThe Rust tool catalog (`crates/shogun-mcp/src/tool_catalog.rs`, E4-T4) is the source. Once E4\nlands, `shogun-mcp --export-tool-table` (name to be confirmed by E4) prints this JSON and CI\nregenerates `apps/api/fixtures/tool-table.json` from it; a test fails when the committed file\ndiffers from the export. **Until then** the committed fixture is hand-maintained to the plan's\n§7.1 list and is marked `\"source\": \"fixture\"`. Nothing else in the repository may list tool names\nor annotations by hand.\n\n## Shape (`shogunagent/tool-table/v1`)\n\n```json\n{\n  \"schema\": \"shogunagent/tool-table/v1\",\n  \"source\": \"shogun-mcp --export-tool-table\" | \"fixture\",\n  \"protocol_version\": \"2026-07-28\",\n  \"toolset\": \"core\",\n  \"tools\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"state.commitments.list\",\n      \"title\": \"List commitments\",\n      \"description\": \"…written for agents: the task, the exclusions, the cost class…\",\n      \"level\": \"read\" | \"L1\" | \"L2\" | \"L3\",\n      \"cost_unit\": \"read\" | \"ingest\" | \"model_op\" | \"run\",\n      \"input_schema\": { \"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": { … }, \"required\": [ … ] },\n      \"output_schema\": { … } | null,\n      \"deprecated\": null | { \"since\": \"0.2.0\", \"replacement\": \"…\", \"removal_after\": \"2027-10-01\" }\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n| Field | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `name` | The frozen wire name (invariant 11). Never renamed in place. |\n| `title` | Short human label (MCP `title`). |\n| `description` | The agent-facing description E4 writes: task, exclusions, cost class. |\n| `level` | `read` for a read; otherwise the permission tier of the write (`L1` / `L2` / `L3`). For `actions.execute` the tier depends on the action type at run time, so the table carries the **highest** tier the tool can reach (`L3`). |\n| `cost_unit` | The metering unit of `apps/api/src/pricing.ts` (`classifyCall`). A test checks the two agree. |\n| `input_schema` | JSON Schema of the tool arguments, as `tools/list` returns it. |\n| `output_schema` | JSON Schema of `structuredContent`, or `null`. |\n| `deprecated` | `null`, or the entry the deprecation policy (`docs/api/deprecation-policy.md`, E4-T5) requires. |\n\n## Annotations are derived, never stored\n\nMCP tool annotations are computed from `level` by one rule (`apps/api/src/wellknown/tool-table.ts`\n`annotationsFor`):\n\n| `level` | `readOnlyHint` | `destructiveHint` | `idempotentHint` | `openWorldHint` |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `read` | `true` | `false` | `true` | `false` |\n| `L1` | `false` | `false` | `true` | `false` |\n| `L2` | `false` | `false` | `false` | `false` |\n| `L3` | `false` | `true` | `false` | `true` |\n\n`L3` is the only tier that leaves the device (invariant 3), so it is the only one with\n`openWorldHint: true`. The SKILL.md sentence \"`readOnlyHint: true` is L1; `destructiveHint: true`\nwith `openWorldHint: true` is L3\" is a consequence of this table, not a second source.\n\n## Validation\n\n`validateToolTable` (same module) rejects: a missing or wrong `schema`, a duplicate name, a name\nthat is not `<group>.<verb>` in lowercase, a `level` or `cost_unit` outside the enums, and an\n`input_schema` that is not an object schema. The CI test `wellknown.test.ts` also asserts the\nfixture's names equal the §7.1 list of `docs/implementation-plan.md`.\n";
